KNOWNBUG
main.cpp
--cpp17
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ call: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
Root cause: when enable_if_t<...> is instantiated here, its body
`typename enable_if<B, T>::type' is resolved in the scope of the template's
declaration (the global namespace), not in a scope holding the template
parameters, so `B' finds the namespace-scope `using B = binder<...>'
("symbol 'B' does not uniquely resolve"), deduction fails and R2 is
unknown.  Renaming the global B makes the test pass.  The fix belongs in
instantiate_template's handling of alias and function templates, which
must look template parameters up before the enclosing scope.  Found while
reducing the MSVC std::function-from-std::bind failure
(cpp11_std_bind_to_std_function); that failure persists when MSVC's
enable_if_t parameters are renamed, so it has another cause.
